Track 3: Zaman-Strut at High Noon
- Story & Context: Nomads on the red dunes swear that whenever two rival trade caravans meet at the dry riverbed of Wadi-Zaman and argue over whose sun-dial is accurate, the phantom band of Old Man Jamil appears to settle the calendar with a blistering hoedown. Witnesses describe the scent of wild sagebrush and sizzling electrical solder blowing across the baking white sands just before a pair of dreadnought guitars launch into a blinding 160-BPM rhythm. The legend insists the phantom musicians play until both caravans forget their quarrels and dance barefoot across the salt crust, Jamil’s voice ringing across the desert wind: "High noon is whenever our fingers catch fire!" To this day, whenever coastal caravans pack away their merchant tents at the midday chime, they strum this exact tune to bless the trail ahead.
